        "question": {
          ":question/problem": "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=-8\\\\ 2x+8y &=-16.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -16 \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -16 \\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=-8\\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+4y=-8.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-4y-8,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4y-8\\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix} -4y-8 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
